# .gitignore — this fork's own ignore rules.
#
# Two rules are not optional and neither may be dropped: `zig build` writes the
# local cache beside the package (`.zig-cache/`) and the installed prefix under
# `zig-out/`, and neither belongs in the repository or in the package manifest's
# `paths`. Both patterns are unanchored, so they also cover the standalone
# consumer package's own `consumer/zig-out/`.
.zig-cache/
zig-out/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Upstream ignore conventions, mirrored here so a developer who runs upstream's
# own tooling at this package root does not dirty the checkout.
#
# These are NOT replacements for the vendored ignore files:
# `vendor/re2/.gitignore` and `vendor/abseil-cpp/.gitignore` are upstream bytes,
# they are recorded in UPSTREAM.sha256 group F, and git honours them at their
# own directory levels. Nothing below removes or weakens either of them; every
# one of RE2's six patterns and every one of Abseil's nine is repeated here, so
# both levels say the same thing. A pattern that is kept only while it matches
# nothing is a pattern the next person greps for, finds empty, and deletes.
#
# RE2's rules, from vendor/re2/.gitignore: five of the six (`*.pyc`, `*.orig`,
# `core`, `obj/`, `user.bazelrc`) match no path in this tree today, and the
# sixth is `benchlog.*`, which is dealt with separately below.
*.pyc
*.orig
core
obj/
user.bazelrc

# Abseil's rules, from vendor/abseil-cpp/.gitignore: all nine, with `*.pyc`
# shared with RE2's set above and listed once. `/bazel-*`,
# `--verbose_explanations` and `copts/__pycache__/` are anchored or specific
# upstream and are repeated here unanchored on purpose: at the package root,
# where a developer may run Bazel, that is the directory the rule is about.
bazel-*
MODULE.bazel.lock
--verbose_explanations
build/
*.swp
CMakeLists.txt.user
.vscode/
copts/__pycache__/

# RE2's `benchlog.*` is for the benchmark log a `make bench` run writes into an
# upstream checkout. vendor/re2/benchlog/** is a different thing entirely:
# upstream's own COMMITTED benchmark corpus, six files, four of which are named
# benchlog.*. This rule is kept because it is upstream's, and the four files are
# re-included at the bottom of this file -- but those two things do not compose
# on their own, and the block at the bottom says exactly what does and does not
# work.
benchlog.*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-including vendored material that an upstream rule would swallow.
#
# Four paths are at risk, and this is the whole hazard:
#
#   vendor/re2/benchlog/benchlog.c2
#   vendor/re2/benchlog/benchlog.mini
#   vendor/re2/benchlog/benchlog.r70
#   vendor/re2/benchlog/benchlog.wreck
#
# They are upstream bytes, group F of UPSTREAM.sha256 records them, and
# `package.nix`'s source allow-list names them through `./vendor`. Dropped from
# a commit, the tree then fails its own integrity check on a correctly fetched
# copy: the manifest records a path the checkout does not have, and
# tools/check-provenance.sh's file-set comparison is exactly what sees it.
#
# The two negations below are correct in intent and are what a tool that reads
# only this file sees. They do not on their own re-include the four files, and
# this comment says so rather than leaving a reader to discover it:
#
#   1. Precedence. Patterns from a lower-level file override those from
#      higher-level files, so `vendor/re2/.gitignore`'s `benchlog.*` outranks
#      whatever this file says. That vendored file is upstream bytes and the tree
#      is pristine, so it cannot be edited to make room, and a negation here
#      cannot outrank it.
#   2. The second negation is inert for a second reason as well: `**` walks into
#      directories, and `vendor/re2/benchlog` was never excluded in the first
#      place, because `benchlog.*` needs a dot and does not match the bare name
#      `benchlog`. There is no excluded directory to descend past, which is also
#      why the risk is four files and not a whole subtree.
#
# What actually works is one force-add, once:
#
#     git add -f vendor/re2/benchlog
#
# the directory, which pulls the four files in -- not four separate paths. After
# that they are tracked, and .gitignore does not apply to tracked paths at all,
# so this is a one-time action rather than something to repeat at every commit.
# Until it happens, those four files are the one part of this repository that a
# plain `git add .` silently drops.
!vendor/re2/benchlog/
!vendor/re2/benchlog/**
